package teyru

/* java.util.stream, in Teyru.
 *
 * Stream, IntStream, LongStream, DoubleStream, Collectors, Collector,
 * Spliterator, Spliterators, StreamSupport -- the sequential half of the
 * package. There is no parallelism anywhere in this dialect (no threads), so
 * `parallel`, `parallelStream` and `StreamSupport.stream(.., true)` all hand
 * back a sequential stream and `isParallel()` is false. Everything else is the
 * JDK's contract, including laziness, which is not a detail here: a stream is
 * a pull pipeline, an intermediate operation only wraps the iterator the
 * previous stage opens, and nothing at all happens until a terminal operation
 * starts asking. `Stream.iterate(1, n -> n * 2).limit(5)` terminates because of
 * that, and so does `generate(..).findFirst()`.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ the shape
 *
 * `list.stream()` is declared where Java declares it, on Collection, in
 * lib/08_collections.teyru; that default method calls `Streams.of(this)`,
 * which is this file's door in. `Streams` also spells the two entry points
 * Java reads off a collection and this dialect cannot reach that way:
 * `Streams.of(collection)` and `Streams.parallelStream(collection)` (the
 * second hands back a sequential stream, like everything here). Every other
 * entry point the JDK has (Stream.of, IntStream.range, StreamSupport,
 * Arrays.stream) is here under its own name.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------ the two extra members
 *
 * Stream is an interface with two members the JDK does not have -- source(),
 * state() -- and one nested type that it does (Stream.Builder). They are the
 * seam the default methods are written against: a stream is "a way to open an
 * iterator" plus the shared state of one pipeline, and every operation in this
 * file is a default method over those two. Without them the whole file would
 * be a second copy of itself, because Teyru has no way to declare a method on
 * someone else's interface and no mixin to hang one on.
 *
 * StreamState carries Java's consume-once rule. Every stream derived from one
 * source shares the state object, so consuming a derived stream consumes the
 * source too -- exactly what the JDK does, and the reason
 * `s.filter(..).count()` followed by `s.count()` throws IllegalStateException.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------- wildcards, in and out
 *
 * Stream's own signatures are written the way the JDK writes them, wildcards
 * included (`Predicate<? super T>`, `Function<? super T, ? extends R>`). A
 * lambda as an argument type-checks against those the way Java does, which is
 * the point. Inside the implementations the wildcards are cast away to the
 * invariant spelling once, at the top of the operation (`Predicate<T> p =
 * (Predicate<T>) predicate`), because a wildcard capture that stays live
 * through five levels of generic plumbing is where this dialect stops being
 * able to follow you. The cast costs nothing: both are the same erased type.
 *
 * Collectors is written the JDK's way too (`Function<? super T, ? extends K>`,
 * `Collector<? super T, A, R>`), with one thing left to know: a lambda
 * argument loses its parameter type when a sibling argument is an inline
 * generic call, so the two-argument groupingBy wants its downstream collector
 * in a variable first:
 *
 *     Collector<String, LongAccumulator, Long> countOf = Collectors.<String>counting()
 *     s.collect(Collectors.<String, Integer, LongAccumulator, Long>groupingBy(x -> x.length(), countOf))
 *
 * The same goes for a lambda whose own return type is an inferred generic call
 * (`w -> Stream.of(w.split(" "))` wants a `Function<String, Stream<String>>`
 * around it). Neither is a signature problem -- the JDK's spelling is what is
 * declared -- and both are one variable away from the Java text.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------- what this file cannot do
 *
 * Teyru does not infer a generic method's type arguments from a lambda's body
 * (docs/language.md §13), and a generic method whose type parameter appears
 * only in its return type has nothing to infer from at all. So
 * `Collectors.toList()` needs the type witness Java only needs when it is
 * ambiguous:
 *
 *     Stream<String> s = ...
 *     s.collect(Collectors.<String>toList())     // not Collectors.toList()
 *     s.collect(Collectors.<String, Integer>groupingBy(x -> x.length()))
 *
 * The same call without the witness is legal Java, so a Java program that uses
 * one is still a Teyru program -- it just spells out what javac was guessing.
 * Stream.toList() (Java 16) is here as well and needs no witness at all.
 *
 * Collectors' return types are spelled with the accumulator made concrete
 * (`Collector<T, List<T>, List<T>>` where the JDK has `Collector<T, ?, List<T>>`)
 * for the same reason: `collect(Collector<? super T, A, R>)` infers A and R
 * from the argument, and a `?` there would leave the inference with nothing to
 * resolve against. A collector that was written to the JDK's spelling still
 * works with `collect`; only a caller who names the accumulator type sees the
 * difference.
 *
 * The primitive streams are final classes rather than interfaces (nothing
 * implements IntStream, here or usefully anywhere) and their elements are boxed
 * Integer/Long/Double inside a Stream, so `IntStream.sum()` unboxes per
 * element. Correct and lazy; not what a JIT-compiled primitive pipeline costs.
 *
 * --------------------------------------------------------- other differences
 *
 *   - Comparator belongs to lib/09_functional.teyru, not to this file. The
 *     comparator-taking methods here (`sorted(cmp)`, `min(cmp)`, `max(cmp)`,
 *     `minBy(cmp)`, `maxBy(cmp)`) hand on whatever comparator they are given,
 *     and the factories a caller writes -- `Comparator.naturalOrder()`,
 *     `Comparator.comparing(..)` -- are declared there.
 *   - No stage of a pipeline reports SIZED or SUBSIZED. A spliterator taken
 *     from a stream is ORDERED and nothing else, and its estimateSize() is
 *     Long.MAX_VALUE: a stage cannot know how many elements are coming without
 *     consuming the source underneath it, which is the one thing a lazy
 *     pipeline may not do. Spliterators.spliterator(..) does report the size
 *     and the flags it was handed.
 *   - trySplit() returns null everywhere, with or without SIZED. There is one
 *     thread here, so there is nothing a split would be for.
 *   - DoubleStream.sum() is a plain sum. Java's is Kahan-compensated, so a
 *     pipeline whose additions lose bits can differ in the last place.
 */

// ------------------------------------------ java.util.function, the rest of it

/* The functional interfaces java.util.stream is declared over and
   lib/09_functional.teyru does not have. They are here rather than there
   because that file is another file's to edit, which is the same reason
   lib/14_util_objects.teyru declares BiConsumer.

   UnaryOperator and BinaryOperator extend Function and BiFunction, as in the
   JDK, so a UnaryOperator<T> is a Function<T, T>. A lambda written against
   either one still sees the substituted parameter type -- `UnaryOperator<T>`
   passes T down to `Function<T, T>`, and a lambda assigned to
   `UnaryOperator<Integer>` sees Integer -- which is what makes the ordinary
   `iterate(0, n -> n + 1)` type-check. */
interface UnaryOperator<T> extends Function<T, T> {
}

interface BinaryOperator<T> extends BiFunction<T, T, T> {
}

interface ToIntFunction<T> {
  int applyAsInt(T value)
}

interface ToLongFunction<T> {
  long applyAsLong(T value)
}

interface ToDoubleFunction<T> {
  double applyAsDouble(T value)
}

interface IntPredicate {
  boolean test(int value)
}

interface IntUnaryOperator {
  int applyAsInt(int operand)
}

interface IntBinaryOperator {
  int applyAsInt(int left, int right)
}

interface IntConsumer {
  void accept(int value)
}

interface IntFunction<R> {
  R apply(int value)
}

interface IntSupplier {
  int getAsInt()
}

interface LongPredicate {
  boolean test(long value)
}

interface LongUnaryOperator {
  long applyAsLong(long operand)
}

interface LongBinaryOperator {
  long applyAsLong(long left, long right)
}

interface LongConsumer {
  void accept(long value)
}

interface LongFunction<R> {
  R apply(long value)
}

interface LongSupplier {
  long getAsLong()
}

interface DoublePredicate {
  boolean test(double value)
}

interface DoubleUnaryOperator {
  double applyAsDouble(double operand)
}

interface DoubleBinaryOperator {
  double applyAsDouble(double left, double right)
}

interface DoubleConsumer {
  void accept(double value)
}

interface DoubleFunction<R> {
  R apply(double value)
}

interface DoubleSupplier {
  double getAsDouble()
}

// ------------------------------------------------------ java.util.Spliterator

/* The half of Spliterator that means anything without parallelism. trySplit
   always answers null -- the JDK's own answer for "this one cannot be split",
   and the only truthful one here -- and every characteristic is a constant on
   this interface, exactly as in the JDK, so `Spliterator.SIZED | ORDERED`
   reads the same in both languages. */
interface Spliterator<T> {
  int DISTINCT = 0x00000001
  int SORTED = 0x00000004
  int ORDERED = 0x00000010
  int SIZED = 0x00000040
  int NONNULL = 0x00000100
  int IMMUTABLE = 0x00000400
  int CONCURRENT = 0x00001000
  int SUBSIZED = 0x00004000

  boolean tryAdvance(Consumer<? super T> action)

  default void forEachRemaining(Consumer<? super T> action) {
    while (tryAdvance(action)) {
    }
  }

  Spliterator<T> trySplit()

  long estimateSize()

  default long getExactSizeIfKnown() {
    return (characteristics() & SIZED) == 0 ? -1 : estimateSize()
  }

  int characteristics()

  default boolean hasCharacteristics(int characteristics) {
    return (characteristics() & characteristics) == characteristics
  }

  /* Java's answer for a spliterator whose SORTED characteristic is not set,
     and for one that is sorted in the natural order. */
  default Comparator<? super T> getComparator() {
    if (hasCharacteristics(SORTED)) {
      return null
    }
    throw new IllegalStateException()
  }
}

// -------------------------------------------- java.util.stream.Stream, and it
//
// Declared before the classes that implement it and before the primitive
// streams it returns, because the C backend writes a class's interface table
// next to the class and every supertype has to be in hand by then.

interface Stream<T> extends AutoCloseable {
  /* The seam: how to open this pipeline's elements, and the state the whole
     pipeline shares. Not part of the JDK; see the header. */
  Supplier<Iterator<T>> source()

  StreamState state()

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ the sources

  static <T> Stream.Builder<T> builder() {
    return new StreamBuilder<T>()
  }

  static <T> Stream<T> empty() {
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new EmptyIterator<T>(), new StreamState())
  }

  static <T> Stream<T> of(T t) {
    return Streams.of(Collections.singletonList(t))
  }

  /* Java has both of(T) and of(T...); so does this, and a one-argument call
     picks the first, which is what javac does too. The array is copied into a
     list rather than viewed through Arrays.asList, because a stream reads its
     source once and never writes it: the two are the same stream. */
  static <T> Stream<T> of(T... values) {
    List<T> xs = new ArrayList<T>(values.length)
    for (int i = 0 : i < values.length : i++) {
      xs.add(values[i])
    }
    return Streams.of(xs)
  }

  static <T> Stream<T> ofNullable(T t) {
    if (t == null) {
      return Stream.empty()
    }
    return Stream.of(t)
  }

  /* The infinite one. Java's two-argument iterate has no hasNext, so its only
     use is with limit/takeWhile/findFirst: this is a stream that answers true
     from hasNext forever, and the operations downstream of it are what stop
     the walk. */
  static <T> Stream<T> iterate(T seed, UnaryOperator<T> f) {
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamIterateIterator<T>(seed, f), new StreamState())
  }

  static <T> Stream<T> iterate(T seed, Predicate<? super T> hasNext, UnaryOperator<T> next) {
    Predicate<T> stop = (Predicate<T>) hasNext
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamIterateWhileIterator<T>(seed, stop, next), new StreamState())
  }

  static <T> Stream<T> generate(Supplier<? extends T> s) {
    Supplier<T> sup = (Supplier<T>) s
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamGenerateIterator<T>(sup), new StreamState())
  }

  static <T> Stream<T> concat(Stream<T> a, Stream<T> b) {
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> first = a.source()
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> second = b.source()
    StreamState shared = new StreamState()
    /* Both sides are consumed by the concatenation, which is what Java does:
       the result's close() closes both. */
    a.state().markUsed()
    b.state().markUsed()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamConcatIterator<T>(first.get(), second.get()), shared)
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------- intermediate operations

  default Stream<T> filter(Predicate<? super T> predicate) {
    Predicate<T> p = (Predicate<T>) predicate
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamFilterIterator<T>(up.get(), p), state())
  }

  default <R> Stream<R> map(Function<? super T, ? extends R> mapper) {
    Function<T, R> m = (Function<T, R>) mapper
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<R>(() -> new StreamMapIterator<T, R>(up.get(), m), state())
  }

  default IntStream mapToInt(ToIntFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToIntFunction<T> m = (ToIntFunction<T>) mapper
    return new IntStream(map(mapperOfInt(m)))
  }

  default LongStream mapToLong(ToLongFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToLongFunction<T> m = (ToLongFunction<T>) mapper
    return new LongStream(map(mapperOfLong(m)))
  }

  default DoubleStream mapToDouble(ToDoubleFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToDoubleFunction<T> m = (ToDoubleFunction<T>) mapper
    return new DoubleStream(map(mapperOfDouble(m)))
  }

  default <R> Stream<R> flatMap(Function<? super T, ? extends Stream<? extends R>> mapper) {
    Function<T, Stream<R>> m = (Function<T, Stream<R>>) mapper
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<R>(() -> new StreamFlatMapIterator<T, R>(up.get(), m), state())
  }

  default Stream<T> distinct() {
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamDistinctIterator<T>(up.get()), state())
  }

  /* Both arities buffer the whole upstream before the first element comes out,
     which is what makes them stateful and lazy at once: nothing is read until
     a terminal operation asks for the first element. */
  default Stream<T> sorted() {
    return sorted(new NaturalOrderComparator<T>())
  }

  default Stream<T> sorted(Comparator<? super T> comparator) {
    Comparator<T> c = (Comparator<T>) comparator
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamSortedIterator<T>(up.get(), c), state())
  }

  default Stream<T> peek(Consumer<? super T> action) {
    Consumer<T> a = (Consumer<T>) action
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamPeekIterator<T>(up.get(), a), state())
  }

  default Stream<T> limit(long maxSize) {
    if (maxSize < 0) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException(Long.toString(maxSize))
    }
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamLimitIterator<T>(up.get(), maxSize), state())
  }

  default Stream<T> skip(long n) {
    if (n < 0) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException(Long.toString(n))
    }
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamSkipIterator<T>(up.get(), n), state())
  }

  default Stream<T> takeWhile(Predicate<? super T> predicate) {
    Predicate<T> p = (Predicate<T>) predicate
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamTakeWhileIterator<T>(up.get(), p), state())
  }

  default Stream<T> dropWhile(Predicate<? super T> predicate) {
    Predicate<T> p = (Predicate<T>) predicate
    Supplier<Iterator<T>> up = source()
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> new StreamDropWhileIterator<T>(up.get(), p), state())
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------- terminal operations

  default void forEach(Consumer<? super T> action) {
    Consumer<T> a = (Consumer<T>) action
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      a.accept(it.next())
    }
  }

  /* The same walk. There is no encounter order to lose without parallelism,
     so the two methods are one implementation, as they are in the JDK for a
     sequential stream. */
  default void forEachOrdered(Consumer<? super T> action) {
    forEach(action)
  }

  default Object[] toArray() {
    List<Object> out = new ArrayList<Object>()
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      out.add(it.next())
    }
    return out.toArray()
  }

  /* Java 16's Stream.toList: a snapshot, unmodifiable. Java's own contract is
     "an unmodifiable List", and it allows nulls, unlike toUnmodifiableList. */
  default List<T> toList() {
    List<T> out = new ArrayList<T>()
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      out.add(it.next())
    }
    return Collections.unmodifiableList(out)
  }

  default T reduce(T identity, BinaryOperator<T> accumulator) {
    T result = identity
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = accumulator.apply(result, it.next())
    }
    return result
  }

  default Optional<T> reduce(BinaryOperator<T> accumulator) {
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return Optional.<T>empty()
    }
    T result = it.next()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = accumulator.apply(result, it.next())
    }
    return Optional.<T>of(result)
  }

  /* The combiner is never called: it exists for the parallel case, and there
     is no parallel case here. Java's sequential implementation does exactly
     this and no more. */
  default <U> U reduce(U identity, BiFunction<U, ? super T, U> accumulator, BinaryOperator<U> combiner) {
    BiFunction<U, T, U> acc = (BiFunction<U, T, U>) accumulator
    U result = identity
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = acc.apply(result, it.next())
    }
    return result
  }

  /* Same story: supplier and accumulator run, the combiner is the parallel
     case's. */
  default <R> R collect(Supplier<R> supplier, BiConsumer<R, ? super T> accumulator, BiConsumer<R, R> combiner) {
    BiConsumer<R, T> acc = (BiConsumer<R, T>) accumulator
    R container = supplier.get()
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      acc.accept(container, it.next())
    }
    return container
  }

  default <A, R> R collect(Collector<? super T, A, R> collector) {
    Supplier<A> supplier = collector.supplier()
    BiConsumer<A, ? super T> sink = collector.accumulator()
    A container = supplier.get()
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      sink.accept(container, it.next())
    }
    return collector.finisher().apply(container)
  }

  default long count() {
    long n = 0
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      it.next()
      n++
    }
    return n
  }

  default Optional<T> min(Comparator<? super T> comparator) {
    return extremum((Comparator<T>) comparator, -1)
  }

  default Optional<T> max(Comparator<? super T> comparator) {
    return extremum((Comparator<T>) comparator, 1)
  }

  default Optional<T> findFirst() {
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    if (it.hasNext()) {
      return Optional.<T>of(it.next())
    }
    return Optional.<T>empty()
  }

  /* The same element as findFirst, and the same one Java's sequential streams
     pick. */
  default Optional<T> findAny() {
    return findFirst()
  }

  default boolean anyMatch(Predicate<? super T> predicate) {
    Predicate<T> p = (Predicate<T>) predicate
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      if (p.test(it.next())) {
        return true
      }
    }
    return false
  }

  default boolean allMatch(Predicate<? super T> predicate) {
    Predicate<T> p = (Predicate<T>) predicate
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      if (!p.test(it.next())) {
        return false
      }
    }
    return true
  }

  default boolean noneMatch(Predicate<? super T> predicate) {
    Predicate<T> p = (Predicate<T>) predicate
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      if (p.test(it.next())) {
        return false
      }
    }
    return true
  }

  // -------------------------------------------- BaseStream, minus parallelism

  /* Consuming the pipeline is what makes the second call throw, and it happens
     here rather than in each terminal operation: they all come through here. */
  default Iterator<T> iterator() {
    state().consume()
    return source().get()
  }

  default Spliterator<T> spliterator() {
    state().consume()
    return new IteratorSpliterator<T>(source().get(), Spliterator.ORDERED)
  }

  default boolean isParallel() {
    return false
  }

  default Stream<T> sequential() {
    return this
  }

  /* Answers a stream that is still sequential: there are no threads in this
     dialect, so there is nothing else it could answer truthfully. The method
     is here because a program that says `parallel()` should run, not fail. */
  default Stream<T> parallel() {
    return this
  }

  default Stream<T> unordered() {
    return this
  }

  default Stream<T> onClose(Runnable closeHandler) {
    state().addCloser(closeHandler)
    return this
  }

  default void close() {
    state().close()
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------- the small print

  private Optional<T> extremum(Comparator<T> comparator, int sign) {
    Iterator<T> it = iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return Optional.<T>empty()
    }
    T best = it.next()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      T e = it.next()
      int c = comparator.compare(e, best)
      // strictly better only, so the first of equals wins, as in Java
      if (sign < 0 ? c < 0 : c > 0) {
        best = e
      }
    }
    return Optional.<T>of(best)
  }

  private Function<T, Integer> mapperOfInt(ToIntFunction<T> f) {
    return t -> Integer.valueOf(f.applyAsInt(t))
  }

  private Function<T, Long> mapperOfLong(ToLongFunction<T> f) {
    return t -> Long.valueOf(f.applyAsLong(t))
  }

  private Function<T, Double> mapperOfDouble(ToDoubleFunction<T> f) {
    return t -> Double.valueOf(f.applyAsDouble(t))
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ Stream.Builder

  /* Java's Builder is a Consumer whose accept means add, and build() is the
     terminal step. It is a nested interface in the JDK, so it is one here. */
  interface Builder<T> extends Consumer<T> {
    default Builder<T> add(T t) {
      accept(t)
      return this
    }

    Stream<T> build()
  }
}

/* The state one pipeline shares: Java's consume-once rule, and the close
   handlers. Every stream an operation derives shares the state of the stream
   it came from, which is what makes
   `s.filter(..).count()` followed by `s.count()` throw, exactly as in the JDK.

   Not part of java.util.stream; see the header for why Stream needs it. */
class StreamState {
  private boolean used
  private List<Runnable> closers = new ArrayList<Runnable>()

  /* Marks the pipeline consumed, or throws if it already was. Java's message
     and Java's rule: the second terminal operation on one pipeline dies. */
  public void consume() {
    if (used) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("stream has already been operated upon or closed")
    }
    used = true
  }

  /* For concat, which consumes both of its inputs up front the way the JDK's
     does, without the exception a second terminal operation would raise. */
  public void markUsed() {
    used = true
  }

  public void addCloser(Runnable action) {
    closers.add(action)
  }

  /* Java's close: every handler runs, in registration order, and the pipeline
     is consumed whether or not it was before. It does not throw on a stream
     that was already consumed -- only the terminal operations do. */
  public void close() {
    used = true
    for (int i = 0 : i < closers.size() : i++) {
      closers.get(i).run()
    }
  }
}

/* The one class that implements the seam. Every operation in Stream returns
   one of these wrapped around a supplier that opens the next stage's
   iterator. */
class StreamImpl<T> implements Stream<T> {
  private Supplier<Iterator<T>> src
  private StreamState shared

  public StreamImpl(Supplier<Iterator<T>> source, StreamState state) {
    src = source
    shared = state
  }

  public Supplier<Iterator<T>> source() {
    return src
  }

  public StreamState state() {
    return shared
  }
}

/* Stream.Builder's implementation. */
class StreamBuilder<T> implements Stream.Builder<T>, Consumer<T> {
  private List<T> xs = new ArrayList<T>()

  public void accept(T t) {
    xs.add(t)
  }

  public Stream<T> build() {
    return Streams.of(xs)
  }
}

/* Spliterators over an iterator. `characteristics` is whatever the factory
   that made it said; nothing here ever splits, so estimateSize is the size the
   caller claimed or the JDK's "unknown" sentinel. */
class IteratorSpliterator<T> implements Spliterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> it
  private int chars
  private long size

  public IteratorSpliterator(Iterator<T> iterator, int characteristics) {
    it = iterator
    chars = characteristics
    size = Long.MAX_VALUE
  }

  public IteratorSpliterator(Iterator<T> iterator, long estimated, int characteristics) {
    it = iterator
    chars = characteristics
    size = estimated
  }

  public boolean tryAdvance(Consumer<? super T> action) {
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return false
    }
    action.accept(it.next())
    return true
  }

  /* null is the JDK's answer for "this spliterator cannot be split", and with
     no threads there is nothing to split for. */
  public Spliterator<T> trySplit() {
    return null
  }

  public long estimateSize() {
    return size
  }

  public int characteristics() {
    return chars
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- java.util.Spliterators

final class Spliterators {
  private Spliterators() {
  }

  public static <T> Spliterator<T> emptySpliterator() {
    return new IteratorSpliterator<T>(new EmptyIterator<T>(), 0)
  }

  public static <T> Spliterator<T> spliterator(Iterator<T> iterator, long size, int characteristics) {
    return new IteratorSpliterator<T>(iterator, size, characteristics)
  }

  public static <T> Spliterator<T> spliteratorUnknownSize(Iterator<T> iterator, int characteristics) {
    return new IteratorSpliterator<T>(iterator, characteristics)
  }

  public static <T> Spliterator<T> spliterator(Object[] array, int characteristics) {
    return new IteratorSpliterator<T>(Spliterators.<T>iteratorsOf(array), array.length, characteristics)
  }

  public static <T> Spliterator<T> spliterator(Object[] array, int fromIndex, int toIndex, int characteristics) {
    return new IteratorSpliterator<T>(Spliterators.<T>iteratorsOf(array, fromIndex, toIndex), toIndex - fromIndex, characteristics)
  }

  /* Java 9's Spliterators.iterator: the spliterator becomes an iterator. Each
     call to next() is one tryAdvance with a one-slot consumer, which is the
     same thing written the long way round. */
  public static <T> Iterator<T> iterator(Spliterator<T> spliterator) {
    return new SpliteratorIterator<T>(spliterator)
  }

  private static <T> Iterator<T> iteratorsOf(Object[] array) {
    List<T> xs = new ArrayList<T>(array.length)
    for (int i = 0 : i < array.length : i++) {
      xs.add((T) array[i])
    }
    return xs.iterator()
  }

  private static <T> Iterator<T> iteratorsOf(Object[] array, int from, int to) {
    List<T> xs = new ArrayList<T>(to - from)
    for (int i = from : i < to : i++) {
      xs.add((T) array[i])
    }
    return xs.iterator()
  }
}

class SpliteratorIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Spliterator<T> src
  private boolean ready
  private T current

  public SpliteratorIterator(Spliterator<T> spliterator) {
    src = spliterator
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (!ready) {
      current = null
      ready = src.tryAdvance(new SpliteratorSink<T>(this))
    }
    return ready
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    ready = false
    return current
  }

  void offer(T value) {
    current = value
  }
}

/* The one-slot consumer SpliteratorIterator hands to tryAdvance. */
class SpliteratorSink<T> implements Consumer<T> {
  private SpliteratorIterator<T> owner

  public SpliteratorSink(SpliteratorIterator<T> target) {
    owner = target
  }

  public void accept(T t) {
    owner.offer(t)
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- java.util.stream.StreamSupport

final class StreamSupport {
  private StreamSupport() {
  }

  public static <T> Stream<T> stream(Spliterator<T> spliterator, boolean parallel) {
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> Spliterators.iterator(spliterator), new StreamState())
  }

  /* The supplier form exists so that the spliterator (and whatever it holds
     open) is not built until something asks for an element. */
  public static <T> Stream<T> stream(Supplier<Spliterator<T>> supplier, int characteristics, boolean parallel) {
    return new StreamImpl<T>(() -> Spliterators.iterator(supplier.get()), new StreamState())
  }

  public static IntStream intStream(Spliterator<Integer> spliterator, boolean parallel) {
    return new IntStream(StreamSupport.stream(spliterator, parallel))
  }

  public static LongStream longStream(Spliterator<Long> spliterator, boolean parallel) {
    return new LongStream(StreamSupport.stream(spliterator, parallel))
  }

  public static DoubleStream doubleStream(Spliterator<Double> spliterator, boolean parallel) {
    return new DoubleStream(StreamSupport.stream(spliterator, parallel))
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ the entry points

/* What `collection.stream()` would be if this file could add a method to
   java.util.Collection. It cannot -- lib/08_collections.teyru declares that
   interface and this file does not own it -- so the call site is
   Streams.of(list). parallelStream is here under the JDK's name and answers a
   sequential stream, which is the only kind there is. */
final class Streams {
  private Streams() {
  }

  public static <T> Stream<T> of(Collection<T> c) {
    return StreamSupport.stream(() -> Spliterators.spliteratorUnknownSize(c.iterator(), Spliterator.ORDERED | Spliterator.SIZED | Spliterator.SUBSIZED), 0, false)
  }

  /* The array is copied into a list for the same reason Stream.of copies: a
     stream never writes its source, so a view and a copy are one stream. */
  public static <T> Stream<T> of(T[] array) {
    List<T> xs = new ArrayList<T>(array.length)
    for (int i = 0 : i < array.length : i++) {
      xs.add(array[i])
    }
    return of(xs)
  }

  public static <T> Stream<T> parallelStream(Collection<T> c) {
    return of(c)
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------ java.util.OptionalInt and friends

/* The primitive optionals. Not a class of Java's, but the four methods every
   client of IntStream.max() uses, with Java's text: `OptionalInt[3]` and
   `OptionalInt.empty`. */
final class OptionalInt {
  private static final OptionalInt EMPTY = new OptionalInt(false, 0)

  private final boolean present
  private final int value

  private OptionalInt(boolean present, int value) {
    this.present = present
    this.value = value
  }

  public static OptionalInt empty() {
    return EMPTY
  }

  public static OptionalInt of(int v) {
    return new OptionalInt(true, v)
  }

  public int getAsInt() {
    if (!present) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("No value present")
    }
    return value
  }

  public boolean isPresent() {
    return present
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return !present
  }

  public void ifPresent(IntConsumer action) {
    if (present) {
      action.accept(value)
    }
  }

  public void ifPresentOrElse(IntConsumer action, Runnable emptyAction) {
    if (present) {
      action.accept(value)
    } else {
      emptyAction.run()
    }
  }

  public int orElse(int other) {
    return present ? value : other
  }

  public int orElseGet(IntSupplier supplier) {
    return present ? value : supplier.getAsInt()
  }

  public int orElseThrow() {
    if (!present) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("No value present")
    }
    return value
  }

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) {
      return true
    }
    if (!(o instanceof OptionalInt)) {
      return false
    }
    OptionalInt other = (OptionalInt) o
    return present == other.isPresent() && (!present || value == other.getAsInt())
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return present ? Integer.hashCode(value) : 0
  }

  public String toString() {
    return present ? "OptionalInt[" + value + "]" : "OptionalInt.empty"
  }
}

final class OptionalLong {
  private static final OptionalLong EMPTY = new OptionalLong(false, 0L)

  private final boolean present
  private final long value

  private OptionalLong(boolean present, long value) {
    this.present = present
    this.value = value
  }

  public static OptionalLong empty() {
    return EMPTY
  }

  public static OptionalLong of(long v) {
    return new OptionalLong(true, v)
  }

  public long getAsLong() {
    if (!present) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("No value present")
    }
    return value
  }

  public boolean isPresent() {
    return present
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return !present
  }

  public void ifPresent(LongConsumer action) {
    if (present) {
      action.accept(value)
    }
  }

  public void ifPresentOrElse(LongConsumer action, Runnable emptyAction) {
    if (present) {
      action.accept(value)
    } else {
      emptyAction.run()
    }
  }

  public long orElse(long other) {
    return present ? value : other
  }

  public long orElseGet(LongSupplier supplier) {
    return present ? value : supplier.getAsLong()
  }

  public long orElseThrow() {
    if (!present) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("No value present")
    }
    return value
  }

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) {
      return true
    }
    if (!(o instanceof OptionalLong)) {
      return false
    }
    OptionalLong other = (OptionalLong) o
    return present == other.isPresent() && (!present || value == other.getAsLong())
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return present ? Long.hashCode(value) : 0
  }

  public String toString() {
    return present ? "OptionalLong[" + value + "]" : "OptionalLong.empty"
  }
}

final class OptionalDouble {
  private static final OptionalDouble EMPTY = new OptionalDouble(false, 0.0)

  private final boolean present
  private final double value

  private OptionalDouble(boolean present, double value) {
    this.present = present
    this.value = value
  }

  public static OptionalDouble empty() {
    return EMPTY
  }

  public static OptionalDouble of(double v) {
    return new OptionalDouble(true, v)
  }

  public double getAsDouble() {
    if (!present) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("No value present")
    }
    return value
  }

  public boolean isPresent() {
    return present
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return !present
  }

  public void ifPresent(DoubleConsumer action) {
    if (present) {
      action.accept(value)
    }
  }

  public void ifPresentOrElse(DoubleConsumer action, Runnable emptyAction) {
    if (present) {
      action.accept(value)
    } else {
      emptyAction.run()
    }
  }

  public double orElse(double other) {
    return present ? value : other
  }

  public double orElseGet(DoubleSupplier supplier) {
    return present ? value : supplier.getAsDouble()
  }

  public double orElseThrow() {
    if (!present) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("No value present")
    }
    return value
  }

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) {
      return true
    }
    if (!(o instanceof OptionalDouble)) {
      return false
    }
    OptionalDouble other = (OptionalDouble) o
    if (present != other.isPresent()) {
      return false
    }
    // the Double contract: NaN equals NaN here, and -0.0 does not equal 0.0
    return !present || Double.doubleToLongBits(value) == Double.doubleToLongBits(other.getAsDouble())
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return present ? Double.hashCode(value) : 0
  }

  public String toString() {
    return present ? "OptionalDouble[" + value + "]" : "OptionalDouble.empty"
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------- java.util summary statistics

/* Java's four-line text, exactly: `average` is formatted with %f, which is six
   decimals, and the empty case reports Integer.MAX_VALUE as the minimum and
   Integer.MIN_VALUE as the maximum, because that is what the counters start
   at. Both are Java's own output, not an approximation of it. */
class IntSummaryStatistics {
  private long count
  private long sum
  private int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE
  private int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE

  public IntSummaryStatistics() {
  }

  public void accept(int value) {
    count++
    sum += (long) value
    if (value < min) {
      min = value
    }
    if (value > max) {
      max = value
    }
  }

  public void combine(IntSummaryStatistics other) {
    count += other.getCount()
    sum += other.getSum()
    if (other.getMin() < min) {
      min = other.getMin()
    }
    if (other.getMax() > max) {
      max = other.getMax()
    }
  }

  public long getCount() {
    return count
  }

  public long getSum() {
    return sum
  }

  public int getMin() {
    return min
  }

  public int getMax() {
    return max
  }

  public double getAverage() {
    return count == 0 ? 0.0 : (double) sum / (double) count
  }

  public String toString() {
    return String.format("IntSummaryStatistics{count=%d, sum=%d, min=%d, average=%f, max=%d}",
      Long.valueOf(count), Long.valueOf(sum), Integer.valueOf(min), Double.valueOf(getAverage()), Integer.valueOf(max))
  }
}

class LongSummaryStatistics {
  private long count
  private long sum
  private long min = Long.MAX_VALUE
  private long max = Long.MIN_VALUE

  public LongSummaryStatistics() {
  }

  public void accept(long value) {
    count++
    sum += value
    if (value < min) {
      min = value
    }
    if (value > max) {
      max = value
    }
  }

  public void combine(LongSummaryStatistics other) {
    count += other.getCount()
    sum += other.getSum()
    if (other.getMin() < min) {
      min = other.getMin()
    }
    if (other.getMax() > max) {
      max = other.getMax()
    }
  }

  public long getCount() {
    return count
  }

  public long getSum() {
    return sum
  }

  public long getMin() {
    return min
  }

  public long getMax() {
    return max
  }

  public double getAverage() {
    return count == 0 ? 0.0 : (double) sum / (double) count
  }

  public String toString() {
    return String.format("LongSummaryStatistics{count=%d, sum=%d, min=%d, average=%f, max=%d}",
      Long.valueOf(count), Long.valueOf(sum), Long.valueOf(min), Double.valueOf(getAverage()), Long.valueOf(max))
  }
}

/* The sum the JDK keeps for doubles is compensated (Kahan) so that
   `summaryStatistics` on a long pipeline does not drift; this one is a plain
   sum. The count, min and max are Java's, and so is the text. */
class DoubleSummaryStatistics {
  private long count
  private double sum
  private double min = Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  private double max = Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY

  public DoubleSummaryStatistics() {
  }

  public void accept(double value) {
    count++
    sum += value
    if (value < min) {
      min = value
    }
    if (value > max) {
      max = value
    }
  }

  public void combine(DoubleSummaryStatistics other) {
    count += other.getCount()
    sum += other.getSum()
    if (other.getMin() < min) {
      min = other.getMin()
    }
    if (other.getMax() > max) {
      max = other.getMax()
    }
  }

  public long getCount() {
    return count
  }

  public double getSum() {
    return sum
  }

  public double getMin() {
    return min
  }

  public double getMax() {
    return max
  }

  public double getAverage() {
    return count == 0 ? 0.0 : sum / (double) count
  }

  public String toString() {
    return String.format("DoubleSummaryStatistics{count=%d, sum=%f, min=%f, average=%f, max=%f}",
      Long.valueOf(count), Double.valueOf(sum), Double.valueOf(min), Double.valueOf(getAverage()), Double.valueOf(max))
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------- java.util.stream.IntStream

/* IntStream, LongStream and DoubleStream. Each holds the boxed pipeline it was
   built from and unboxes at the boundary; the element type of a generic in
   this dialect cannot be a primitive, so the boxed Stream is where the
   laziness lives and this class is a typed face over it. `elements` has no
   modifier on purpose: Stream.mapToInt, StreamSupport and the other primitive
   streams in this file build one of these around a pipeline they already have,
   and the alternative is a third name in the public surface.

   The one thing worth knowing about the conversions: `mapToInt` on a
   Stream<T> is `map(t -> Integer.valueOf(mapper.applyAsInt(t)))`, so the
   intermediate stage is a Stream<Integer> and the boxing is per element. */
final class IntStream {
  Stream<Integer> elements

  IntStream(Stream<Integer> elements) {
    this.elements = elements
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------- the sources

  public static IntStream empty() {
    return new IntStream(Stream.<Integer>empty())
  }

  public static IntStream of(int t) {
    return of(new int[]{t})
  }

  public static IntStream of(int... values) {
    List<Integer> xs = new ArrayList<Integer>(values.length)
    for (int i = 0 : i < values.length : i++) {
      xs.add(Integer.valueOf(values[i]))
    }
    return new IntStream(Streams.of(xs))
  }

  public static IntStream iterate(int seed, IntUnaryOperator f) {
    return new IntStream(Stream.iterate(Integer.valueOf(seed), n -> Integer.valueOf(f.applyAsInt(n.intValue()))))
  }

  public static IntStream iterate(int seed, IntPredicate hasNext, IntUnaryOperator next) {
    return new IntStream(Stream.iterate(Integer.valueOf(seed), n -> hasNext.test(n.intValue()), n -> Integer.valueOf(next.applyAsInt(n.intValue()))))
  }

  public static IntStream generate(IntSupplier s) {
    return new IntStream(Stream.generate(() -> Integer.valueOf(s.getAsInt())))
  }

  public static IntStream range(int startInclusive, int endExclusive) {
    if (startInclusive >= endExclusive) {
      return empty()
    }
    return iterate(startInclusive, n -> n + 1).limit((long) endExclusive - (long) startInclusive)
  }

  /* `to + 1` in int would wrap for Integer.MAX_VALUE, so the count is taken in
     long, where Java's rangeClosed counts too. */
  public static IntStream rangeClosed(int startInclusive, int endInclusive) {
    if (startInclusive > endInclusive) {
      return empty()
    }
    return iterate(startInclusive, n -> n + 1).limit((long) endInclusive - (long) startInclusive + 1L)
  }

  public static IntStream concat(IntStream a, IntStream b) {
    return new IntStream(Stream.concat(a.elements, b.elements))
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------- intermediate operations

  public IntStream filter(IntPredicate predicate) {
    return new IntStream(elements.filter(v -> predicate.test(v.intValue())))
  }

  public IntStream map(IntUnaryOperator mapper) {
    return new IntStream(elements.map(v -> Integer.valueOf(mapper.applyAsInt(v.intValue()))))
  }

  public <U> Stream<U> mapToObj(IntFunction<? extends U> mapper) {
    IntFunction<U> m = (IntFunction<U>) mapper
    return elements.map(v -> m.apply(v.intValue()))
  }

  public IntStream flatMap(IntFunction<? extends IntStream> mapper) {
    IntFunction<IntStream> m = (IntFunction<IntStream>) mapper
    return new IntStream(elements.flatMap(v -> m.apply(v.intValue()).elements))
  }

  public IntStream distinct() {
    return new IntStream(elements.distinct())
  }

  public IntStream sorted() {
    return new IntStream(elements.sorted())
  }

  public IntStream peek(IntConsumer action) {
    return new IntStream(elements.peek(v -> action.accept(v.intValue())))
  }

  public IntStream limit(long maxSize) {
    return new IntStream(elements.limit(maxSize))
  }

  public IntStream skip(long n) {
    return new IntStream(elements.skip(n))
  }

  public IntStream takeWhile(IntPredicate predicate) {
    return new IntStream(elements.takeWhile(v -> predicate.test(v.intValue())))
  }

  public IntStream dropWhile(IntPredicate predicate) {
    return new IntStream(elements.dropWhile(v -> predicate.test(v.intValue())))
  }

  public LongStream asLongStream() {
    return new LongStream(elements.map(v -> Long.valueOf((long) v.intValue())))
  }

  public DoubleStream asDoubleStream() {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.map(v -> Double.valueOf((double) v.intValue())))
  }

  public Stream<Integer> boxed() {
    return elements
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------- terminal operations

  public void forEach(IntConsumer action) {
    elements.forEach(v -> action.accept(v.intValue()))
  }

  public void forEachOrdered(IntConsumer action) {
    forEach(action)
  }

  public int[] toArray() {
    List<Integer> xs = new ArrayList<Integer>()
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      xs.add(it.next())
    }
    int[] out = new int[xs.size()]
    for (int i = 0 : i < out.length : i++) {
      out[i] = xs.get(i).intValue()
    }
    return out
  }

  public int reduce(int identity, IntBinaryOperator op) {
    int result = identity
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = op.applyAsInt(result, it.next().intValue())
    }
    return result
  }

  public OptionalInt reduce(IntBinaryOperator op) {
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalInt.empty()
    }
    int result = it.next().intValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = op.applyAsInt(result, it.next().intValue())
    }
    return OptionalInt.of(result)
  }

  public <R> R collect(Supplier<R> supplier, ObjIntConsumer<R> accumulator, BiConsumer<R, R> combiner) {
    R container = supplier.get()
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      accumulator.accept(container, it.next().intValue())
    }
    return container
  }

  public int sum() {
    int total = 0
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      total += it.next().intValue()
    }
    return total
  }

  public OptionalInt min() {
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalInt.empty()
    }
    int best = it.next().intValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      int v = it.next().intValue()
      if (v < best) {
        best = v
      }
    }
    return OptionalInt.of(best)
  }

  public OptionalInt max() {
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalInt.empty()
    }
    int best = it.next().intValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      int v = it.next().intValue()
      if (v > best) {
        best = v
      }
    }
    return OptionalInt.of(best)
  }

  public long count() {
    return elements.count()
  }

  /* Java sums the elements in a long here and divides once, which is what
     keeps 2,000,000,000 + 2,000,000,000 from wrapping before the division. */
  public OptionalDouble average() {
    long total = 0
    long n = 0
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      total += (long) it.next().intValue()
      n++
    }
    if (n == 0) {
      return OptionalDouble.empty()
    }
    return OptionalDouble.of((double) total / (double) n)
  }

  public IntSummaryStatistics summaryStatistics() {
    IntSummaryStatistics stats = new IntSummaryStatistics()
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      stats.accept(it.next().intValue())
    }
    return stats
  }

  public boolean anyMatch(IntPredicate predicate) {
    return elements.anyMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.intValue()))
  }

  public boolean allMatch(IntPredicate predicate) {
    return elements.allMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.intValue()))
  }

  public boolean noneMatch(IntPredicate predicate) {
    return elements.noneMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.intValue()))
  }

  public OptionalInt findFirst() {
    Iterator<Integer> it = elements.iterator()
    if (it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalInt.of(it.next().intValue())
    }
    return OptionalInt.empty()
  }

  public OptionalInt findAny() {
    return findFirst()
  }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------- java.util.stream.LongStream

final class LongStream {
  Stream<Long> elements

  LongStream(Stream<Long> elements) {
    this.elements = elements
  }

  public static LongStream empty() {
    return new LongStream(Stream.<Long>empty())
  }

  public static LongStream of(long t) {
    return of(new long[]{t})
  }

  public static LongStream of(long... values) {
    List<Long> xs = new ArrayList<Long>(values.length)
    for (int i = 0 : i < values.length : i++) {
      xs.add(Long.valueOf(values[i]))
    }
    return new LongStream(Streams.of(xs))
  }

  public static LongStream iterate(long seed, LongUnaryOperator f) {
    return new LongStream(Stream.iterate(Long.valueOf(seed), n -> Long.valueOf(f.applyAsLong(n.longValue()))))
  }

  public static LongStream iterate(long seed, LongPredicate hasNext, LongUnaryOperator next) {
    return new LongStream(Stream.iterate(Long.valueOf(seed), n -> hasNext.test(n.longValue()), n -> Long.valueOf(next.applyAsLong(n.longValue()))))
  }

  public static LongStream generate(LongSupplier s) {
    return new LongStream(Stream.generate(() -> Long.valueOf(s.getAsLong())))
  }

  public static LongStream range(long startInclusive, long endExclusive) {
    if (startInclusive >= endExclusive) {
      return empty()
    }
    return iterate(startInclusive, n -> n + 1L).limit(endExclusive - startInclusive)
  }

  public static LongStream rangeClosed(long startInclusive, long endInclusive) {
    if (startInclusive > endInclusive) {
      return empty()
    }
    return iterate(startInclusive, n -> n + 1L).limit(endInclusive - startInclusive + 1L)
  }

  public static LongStream concat(LongStream a, LongStream b) {
    return new LongStream(Stream.concat(a.elements, b.elements))
  }

  public LongStream filter(LongPredicate predicate) {
    return new LongStream(elements.filter(v -> predicate.test(v.longValue())))
  }

  public LongStream map(LongUnaryOperator mapper) {
    return new LongStream(elements.map(v -> Long.valueOf(mapper.applyAsLong(v.longValue()))))
  }

  public <U> Stream<U> mapToObj(LongFunction<? extends U> mapper) {
    LongFunction<U> m = (LongFunction<U>) mapper
    return elements.map(v -> m.apply(v.longValue()))
  }

  public LongStream distinct() {
    return new LongStream(elements.distinct())
  }

  public LongStream sorted() {
    return new LongStream(elements.sorted())
  }

  public LongStream peek(LongConsumer action) {
    return new LongStream(elements.peek(v -> action.accept(v.longValue())))
  }

  public LongStream limit(long maxSize) {
    return new LongStream(elements.limit(maxSize))
  }

  public LongStream skip(long n) {
    return new LongStream(elements.skip(n))
  }

  public LongStream takeWhile(LongPredicate predicate) {
    return new LongStream(elements.takeWhile(v -> predicate.test(v.longValue())))
  }

  public LongStream dropWhile(LongPredicate predicate) {
    return new LongStream(elements.dropWhile(v -> predicate.test(v.longValue())))
  }

  public DoubleStream asDoubleStream() {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.map(v -> Double.valueOf((double) v.longValue())))
  }

  public Stream<Long> boxed() {
    return elements
  }

  public void forEach(LongConsumer action) {
    elements.forEach(v -> action.accept(v.longValue()))
  }

  public void forEachOrdered(LongConsumer action) {
    forEach(action)
  }

  public long[] toArray() {
    List<Long> xs = new ArrayList<Long>()
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      xs.add(it.next())
    }
    long[] out = new long[xs.size()]
    for (int i = 0 : i < out.length : i++) {
      out[i] = xs.get(i).longValue()
    }
    return out
  }

  public long reduce(long identity, LongBinaryOperator op) {
    long result = identity
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = op.applyAsLong(result, it.next().longValue())
    }
    return result
  }

  public OptionalLong reduce(LongBinaryOperator op) {
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalLong.empty()
    }
    long result = it.next().longValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = op.applyAsLong(result, it.next().longValue())
    }
    return OptionalLong.of(result)
  }

  public <R> R collect(Supplier<R> supplier, ObjLongConsumer<R> accumulator, BiConsumer<R, R> combiner) {
    R container = supplier.get()
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      accumulator.accept(container, it.next().longValue())
    }
    return container
  }

  public long sum() {
    long total = 0
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      total += it.next().longValue()
    }
    return total
  }

  public OptionalLong min() {
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalLong.empty()
    }
    long best = it.next().longValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      long v = it.next().longValue()
      if (v < best) {
        best = v
      }
    }
    return OptionalLong.of(best)
  }

  public OptionalLong max() {
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalLong.empty()
    }
    long best = it.next().longValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      long v = it.next().longValue()
      if (v > best) {
        best = v
      }
    }
    return OptionalLong.of(best)
  }

  public long count() {
    return elements.count()
  }

  public OptionalDouble average() {
    return elements.mapToDouble(v -> Double.valueOf((double) v.longValue())).average()
  }

  public LongSummaryStatistics summaryStatistics() {
    LongSummaryStatistics stats = new LongSummaryStatistics()
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      stats.accept(it.next().longValue())
    }
    return stats
  }

  public boolean anyMatch(LongPredicate predicate) {
    return elements.anyMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.longValue()))
  }

  public boolean allMatch(LongPredicate predicate) {
    return elements.allMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.longValue()))
  }

  public boolean noneMatch(LongPredicate predicate) {
    return elements.noneMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.longValue()))
  }

  public OptionalLong findFirst() {
    Iterator<Long> it = elements.iterator()
    if (it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalLong.of(it.next().longValue())
    }
    return OptionalLong.empty()
  }

  public OptionalLong findAny() {
    return findFirst()
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- java.util.stream.DoubleStream

final class DoubleStream {
  Stream<Double> elements

  DoubleStream(Stream<Double> elements) {
    this.elements = elements
  }

  public static DoubleStream empty() {
    return new DoubleStream(Stream.<Double>empty())
  }

  public static DoubleStream of(double t) {
    return of(new double[]{t})
  }

  public static DoubleStream of(double... values) {
    List<Double> xs = new ArrayList<Double>(values.length)
    for (int i = 0 : i < values.length : i++) {
      xs.add(Double.valueOf(values[i]))
    }
    return new DoubleStream(Streams.of(xs))
  }

  public static DoubleStream iterate(double seed, DoubleUnaryOperator f) {
    return new DoubleStream(Stream.iterate(Double.valueOf(seed), n -> Double.valueOf(f.applyAsDouble(n.doubleValue()))))
  }

  public static DoubleStream iterate(double seed, DoublePredicate hasNext, DoubleUnaryOperator next) {
    return new DoubleStream(Stream.iterate(Double.valueOf(seed), n -> hasNext.test(n.doubleValue()), n -> Double.valueOf(next.applyAsDouble(n.doubleValue()))))
  }

  public static DoubleStream generate(DoubleSupplier s) {
    return new DoubleStream(Stream.generate(() -> Double.valueOf(s.getAsDouble())))
  }

  public static DoubleStream concat(DoubleStream a, DoubleStream b) {
    return new DoubleStream(Stream.concat(a.elements, b.elements))
  }

  public DoubleStream filter(DoublePredicate predicate) {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.filter(v -> predicate.test(v.doubleValue())))
  }

  public DoubleStream map(DoubleUnaryOperator mapper) {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.map(v -> Double.valueOf(mapper.applyAsDouble(v.doubleValue()))))
  }

  public <U> Stream<U> mapToObj(DoubleFunction<? extends U> mapper) {
    DoubleFunction<U> m = (DoubleFunction<U>) mapper
    return elements.map(v -> m.apply(v.doubleValue()))
  }

  public DoubleStream distinct() {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.distinct())
  }

  public DoubleStream sorted() {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.sorted())
  }

  public DoubleStream peek(DoubleConsumer action) {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.peek(v -> action.accept(v.doubleValue())))
  }

  public DoubleStream limit(long maxSize) {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.limit(maxSize))
  }

  public DoubleStream skip(long n) {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.skip(n))
  }

  public DoubleStream takeWhile(DoublePredicate predicate) {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.takeWhile(v -> predicate.test(v.doubleValue())))
  }

  public DoubleStream dropWhile(DoublePredicate predicate) {
    return new DoubleStream(elements.dropWhile(v -> predicate.test(v.doubleValue())))
  }

  public Stream<Double> boxed() {
    return elements
  }

  public void forEach(DoubleConsumer action) {
    elements.forEach(v -> action.accept(v.doubleValue()))
  }

  public void forEachOrdered(DoubleConsumer action) {
    forEach(action)
  }

  public double[] toArray() {
    List<Double> xs = new ArrayList<Double>()
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      xs.add(it.next())
    }
    double[] out = new double[xs.size()]
    for (int i = 0 : i < out.length : i++) {
      out[i] = xs.get(i).doubleValue()
    }
    return out
  }

  public double reduce(double identity, DoubleBinaryOperator op) {
    double result = identity
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = op.applyAsDouble(result, it.next().doubleValue())
    }
    return result
  }

  public OptionalDouble reduce(DoubleBinaryOperator op) {
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalDouble.empty()
    }
    double result = it.next().doubleValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      result = op.applyAsDouble(result, it.next().doubleValue())
    }
    return OptionalDouble.of(result)
  }

  public <R> R collect(Supplier<R> supplier, ObjDoubleConsumer<R> accumulator, BiConsumer<R, R> combiner) {
    R container = supplier.get()
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      accumulator.accept(container, it.next().doubleValue())
    }
    return container
  }

  /* A plain sum. Java's DoubleStream.sum is Kahan-compensated, so a pipeline
     where the additions lose bits can differ in the last place. */
  public double sum() {
    double total = 0.0
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      total += it.next().doubleValue()
    }
    return total
  }

  public OptionalDouble min() {
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalDouble.empty()
    }
    double best = it.next().doubleValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      double v = it.next().doubleValue()
      if (v < best) {
        best = v
      }
    }
    return OptionalDouble.of(best)
  }

  public OptionalDouble max() {
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    if (!it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalDouble.empty()
    }
    double best = it.next().doubleValue()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      double v = it.next().doubleValue()
      if (v > best) {
        best = v
      }
    }
    return OptionalDouble.of(best)
  }

  public long count() {
    return elements.count()
  }

  public OptionalDouble average() {
    double total = 0.0
    long n = 0
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      total += it.next().doubleValue()
      n++
    }
    if (n == 0) {
      return OptionalDouble.empty()
    }
    return OptionalDouble.of(total / (double) n)
  }

  public DoubleSummaryStatistics summaryStatistics() {
    DoubleSummaryStatistics stats = new DoubleSummaryStatistics()
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    while (it.hasNext()) {
      stats.accept(it.next().doubleValue())
    }
    return stats
  }

  public boolean anyMatch(DoublePredicate predicate) {
    return elements.anyMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.doubleValue()))
  }

  public boolean allMatch(DoublePredicate predicate) {
    return elements.allMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.doubleValue()))
  }

  public boolean noneMatch(DoublePredicate predicate) {
    return elements.noneMatch(v -> predicate.test(v.doubleValue()))
  }

  public OptionalDouble findFirst() {
    Iterator<Double> it = elements.iterator()
    if (it.hasNext()) {
      return OptionalDouble.of(it.next().doubleValue())
    }
    return OptionalDouble.empty()
  }

  public OptionalDouble findAny() {
    return findFirst()
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- the pipeline stages

/* One class per intermediate operation, each wrapping the upstream iterator.
   This is where the laziness is: a stage does nothing until hasNext() is
   asked, and then it pulls only as far as the answer needs. `peek` after a
   `filter` therefore prints only what the filter let through, and `limit(1)`
   over an infinite `iterate` asks for exactly one element.

   Every one of them is an Iterator<T> and nothing else -- no reset, no
   lookahead the caller can see -- which is what lets Stream.iterator() be one
   line and every terminal operation a while loop. */

class StreamIterateIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private T seed
  private UnaryOperator<T> f
  private boolean first = true

  public StreamIterateIterator(T seed, UnaryOperator<T> f) {
    this.seed = seed
    this.f = f
  }

  /* The seed comes out first, and each later element is f of the one before.
     There is no end: hasNext() is true forever, and `limit` or `takeWhile`
     upstream is what stops the walk. */
  public boolean hasNext() {
    return true
  }

  public T next() {
    if (first) {
      first = false
      return seed
    }
    seed = f.apply(seed)
    return seed
  }
}

class StreamIterateWhileIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private T seed
  private Predicate<T> stop
  private UnaryOperator<T> nextValue
  private boolean alive = true

  public StreamIterateWhileIterator(T seed, Predicate<T> stop, UnaryOperator<T> next) {
    this.seed = seed
    this.stop = stop
    this.nextValue = next
  }

  /* Java 9's three-argument iterate: the current value is tested before it is
     handed out, so `iterate(0, n -> n < 3, n -> n + 1)` is 0, 1, 2 -- three
     elements, where an until-style loop would have given four. The value is
     advanced on the way out of next(), so hasNext() can be called any number
     of times without moving the walk on. */
  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (!alive) {
      return false
    }
    if (!stop.test(seed)) {
      alive = false
      return false
    }
    return true
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    T result = seed
    seed = nextValue.apply(seed)
    return result
  }
}

class StreamGenerateIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Supplier<T> sup

  public StreamGenerateIterator(Supplier<T> sup) {
    this.sup = sup
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    return true
  }

  public T next() {
    return sup.get()
  }
}

class StreamConcatIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> first
  private Iterator<T> second
  private boolean onSecond = false

  public StreamConcatIterator(Iterator<T> first, Iterator<T> second) {
    this.first = first
    this.second = second
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (!onSecond && !first.hasNext()) {
      onSecond = true
    }
    if (onSecond) {
      return second.hasNext()
    }
    return true
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    if (onSecond) {
      return second.next()
    }
    return first.next()
  }
}

class StreamFilterIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Predicate<T> p
  private T pending
  private boolean ready = false

  public StreamFilterIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, Predicate<T> predicate) {
    up = upstream
    p = predicate
  }

  /* The loop keeps pulling until it finds an element the predicate accepts or
     the upstream runs out, so a filtered stream costs exactly the elements it
     has to look at -- the `peek` before the filter sees the rejected ones, the
     one after does not. */
  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (ready) {
      return true
    }
    while (up.hasNext()) {
      T e = up.next()
      if (p.test(e)) {
        pending = e
        ready = true
        return true
      }
    }
    return false
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    T result = pending
    ready = false
    pending = null
    return result
  }
}

class StreamMapIterator<T, R> implements Iterator<R> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Function<T, R> m

  public StreamMapIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, Function<T, R> mapper) {
    up = upstream
    m = mapper
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    return up.hasNext()
  }

  public R next() {
    return m.apply(up.next())
  }
}

/* flatMap: one upstream element at a time, and the inner iterator is drained
   before the next upstream element is asked for. `peek` between the outer
   elements therefore fires when that element's inner stream is opened, which
   is the same moment Java's does. */
class StreamFlatMapIterator<T, R> implements Iterator<R> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Function<T, Stream<R>> m
  private Iterator<R> inner = null

  public StreamFlatMapIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, Function<T, Stream<R>> mapper) {
    up = upstream
    m = mapper
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    while (true) {
      if (inner != null && inner.hasNext()) {
        return true
      }
      if (!up.hasNext()) {
        return false
      }
      inner = m.apply(up.next()).iterator()
    }
  }

  public R next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    return inner.next()
  }
}

/* distinct remembers what it has already handed out, in a HashSet, so the
   memory is proportional to the number of distinct elements rather than to
   the stream. It is still lazy and still works on an infinite stream; the
   `peek` upstream sees every element, the one downstream only the first of
   each. */
class StreamDistinctIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Set<T> seen = new HashSet<T>()
  private T pending
  private boolean ready = false

  public StreamDistinctIterator(Iterator<T> upstream) {
    up = upstream
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (ready) {
      return true
    }
    while (up.hasNext()) {
      T e = up.next()
      if (seen.add(e)) {
        pending = e
        ready = true
        return true
      }
    }
    return false
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    T result = pending
    ready = false
    pending = null
    return result
  }
}

/* sorted buffers everything, then hands out the sorted list. The buffer is
   filled on the first hasNext() rather than in the constructor so that a
   sorted stream nobody asks for still reads nothing. The sort is stable --
   Collections.sort is -- so equal elements keep their encounter order, as in
   Java's sequential sorted(). */
class StreamSortedIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Comparator<T> c
  private Iterator<T> sorted = null

  public StreamSortedIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, Comparator<T> comparator) {
    up = upstream
    c = comparator
  }

  private void fill() {
    List<T> xs = new ArrayList<T>()
    while (up.hasNext()) {
      xs.add(up.next())
    }
    Collections.sort(xs, c)
    sorted = xs.iterator()
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (sorted == null) {
      fill()
    }
    return sorted.hasNext()
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    return sorted.next()
  }
}

class StreamPeekIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Consumer<T> action

  public StreamPeekIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, Consumer<T> a) {
    up = upstream
    action = a
  }

  /* The action runs inside next(), so it fires exactly once per element that
     the rest of the pipeline actually pulls -- and never for an element a
     later stage would have dropped without asking for it. */
  public boolean hasNext() {
    return up.hasNext()
  }

  public T next() {
    T e = up.next()
    action.accept(e)
    return e
  }
}

class StreamLimitIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private long remaining

  public StreamLimitIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, long maxSize) {
    up = upstream
    remaining = maxSize
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (remaining <= 0) {
      return false
    }
    return up.hasNext()
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    remaining--
    return up.next()
  }
}

class StreamSkipIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private long remaining
  private boolean skipped = false

  public StreamSkipIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, long n) {
    up = upstream
    remaining = n
  }

  private void skipAhead() {
    while (remaining > 0 && up.hasNext()) {
      up.next()
      remaining--
    }
    skipped = true
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (!skipped) {
      skipAhead()
    }
    return up.hasNext()
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    return up.next()
  }
}

/* takeWhile: stops at the first element the predicate rejects and never looks
   again, so an infinite upstream is fine as long as the predicate eventually
   says no. That rejected element is consumed -- Java consumes it too, which is
   why `dropWhile` on the same two-clause split has to be its own pass. */
class StreamTakeWhileIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Predicate<T> p
  private T pending
  private boolean ready = false
  private boolean stopped = false

  public StreamTakeWhileIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, Predicate<T> predicate) {
    up = upstream
    p = predicate
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (ready) {
      return true
    }
    if (stopped || !up.hasNext()) {
      return false
    }
    T e = up.next()
    if (!p.test(e)) {
      stopped = true
      return false
    }
    pending = e
    ready = true
    return true
  }

  public T next() {
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    T result = pending
    ready = false
    pending = null
    return result
  }
}

/* dropWhile: throws away the leading run the predicate accepts, then passes
   everything else through untouched -- including later elements the predicate
   would also accept, which is the difference from filter. */
class StreamDropWhileIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
  private Iterator<T> up
  private Predicate<T> p
  private boolean dropped = false
  private T pending
  private boolean ready = false

  public StreamDropWhileIterator(Iterator<T> upstream, Predicate<T> predicate) {
    up = upstream
    p = predicate
  }

  private void dropAhead() {
    while (up.hasNext()) {
      T e = up.next()
      if (!p.test(e)) {
        pending = e
        ready = true
        break
      }
    }
    dropped = true
  }

  public boolean hasNext() {
    if (!dropped) {
      dropAhead()
    }
    return ready || up.hasNext()
  }

  public T next() {
    if (ready) {
      T result = pending
      ready = false
      pending = null
      return result
    }
    if (!hasNext()) {
      throw new NoSuchElementException("no more elements")
    }
    return up.next()
  }
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------- Obj*Consumer

/* java.util.function's three object-plus-primitive consumers. They are here
   for the same reason the rest of that package's tail is: IntStream.collect
   takes one, and lib/09_functional.teyru is another file's. */
interface ObjIntConsumer<T> {
  void accept(T t, int value)
}

interface ObjLongConsumer<T> {
  void accept(T t, long value)
}

interface ObjDoubleConsumer<T> {
  void accept(T t, double value)
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- the accumulators

/* The mutable boxes the collectors accumulate into. Java hides these behind
   `Collector<T, ?, R>`; this dialect cannot write a wildcard there without
   leaving `collect` nothing to infer, so they are named, and they are small.
   `value` is package-visible because that is the whole point of the box. */
class StreamBox<T> {
  T value

  StreamBox(T value) {
    this.value = value
  }
}

class IntAccumulator {
  int value
}

class LongAccumulator {
  long value
}

class DoubleAccumulator {
  double value
}

/* averagingInt and averagingLong sum in a long and count, exactly as Java
   does, so that 2,000,000,000 + 2,000,000,000 averages to 2.0E9 instead of to
   a wrapped int. */
class AverageAccumulator {
  long count
  long sum
}

class DoubleAverageAccumulator {
  long count
  double sum
}

// ------------------------------------------------------ java.util.stream.Collector

/* `Collector<T, A, R>`: how to make a result container, fold one element into
   it, merge two of them, and turn the container into the answer. The combiner
   is the parallel case's -- nothing in this dialect runs it, since there are
   no threads and collect() is a straight fold -- but the interface keeps it in
   the JDK's position and the sequential collectors below still write one that
   works, so a program that calls combiner() itself gets the right answer.

   `characteristics()` answers a Set of the nested enum, as in the JDK. Nothing
   downstream reads it: the IDENTITY_FINISH hint only tells a collector library
   when it may skip the finisher, and with no parallel collect there is nothing
   to tell. It is here because a Collector written against the JDK's interface
   implements it, and dropping it would make the type not a Collector. */
interface Collector<T, A, R> {
  enum Characteristics {
    CONCURRENT,
    UNORDERED,
    IDENTITY_FINISH
  }

  Supplier<A> supplier()

  BiConsumer<A, T> accumulator()

  BinaryOperator<A> combiner()

  Function<A, R> finisher()

  Set<Characteristics> characteristics()

  /* Java 9's two Collector.of factories. The five-argument form is the whole
     contract spelled out; the four-argument one is the same thing with the
     identity function as finisher, which is why A and R are one type there. */
  static <T, A, R> Collector<T, A, R> of(Supplier<A> supplier, BiConsumer<A, T> accumulator, BinaryOperator<A> combiner, Function<A, R> finisher, Characteristics... characteristics) {
    return new CollectorImpl<T, A, R>(supplier, accumulator, combiner, finisher, CollectorChars.of(characteristics))
  }

  static <T, A> Collector<T, A, A> of(Supplier<A> supplier, BiConsumer<A, T> accumulator, BinaryOperator<A> combiner, Characteristics... characteristics) {
    return new CollectorImpl<T, A, A>(supplier, accumulator, combiner, CollectorImpl.<A>identity(), CollectorChars.of(characteristics))
  }
}

/* The characteristics set, and the identity finisher, both small enough that
   spelling them at each collector would be noise. */
final class CollectorChars {
  private CollectorChars() {
  }

  public static Set<Collector.Characteristics> none() {
    return new HashSet<Collector.Characteristics>()
  }

  public static Set<Collector.Characteristics> identityFinish() {
    Set<Collector.Characteristics> s = none()
    s.add(Collector.Characteristics.IDENTITY_FINISH)
    return s
  }

  public static Set<Collector.Characteristics> of(Collector.Characteristics... given) {
    Set<Collector.Characteristics> s = none()
    for (int i = 0 : i < given.length : i++) {
      s.add(given[i])
    }
    return s
  }
}

final class CollectorImpl<T, A, R> implements Collector<T, A, R> {
  private Supplier<A> sup
  private BiConsumer<A, T> acc
  private BinaryOperator<A> comb
  private Function<A, R> fin
  private Set<Collector.Characteristics> chars

  public CollectorImpl(Supplier<A> supplier, BiConsumer<A, T> accumulator, BinaryOperator<A> combiner, Function<A, R> finisher, Set<Collector.Characteristics> characteristics) {
    sup = supplier
    acc = accumulator
    comb = combiner
    fin = finisher
    chars = characteristics
  }

  public Supplier<A> supplier() {
    return sup
  }

  public BiConsumer<A, T> accumulator() {
    return acc
  }

  public BinaryOperator<A> combiner() {
    return comb
  }

  public Function<A, R> finisher() {
    return fin
  }

  public Set<Collector.Characteristics> characteristics() {
    return chars
  }

  /* The finisher of every collector whose container is already its answer. */
  public static <A> Function<A, A> identity() {
    return a -> a
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------ java.util.stream.Collectors

/* The collectors. Each one is a CollectorImpl over four lambdas, which is all
   a collector ever is.

   Two things to know before reading:

   - The return types name the accumulator (`Collector<T, List<T>, List<T>>`)
     where the JDK writes `Collector<T, ?, List<T>>`. See the file header:
     `collect(Collector<? super T, A, R>)` has to infer A, and a wildcard
     leaves it nothing to infer from. A caller who writes the JDK's spelling in
     a variable declaration still compiles -- `?` accepts the concrete type.

   - A type witness is needed at the call site for the collectors whose type
     parameters cannot be read off the arguments, because this dialect will not
     infer from a lambda's body:
     `Collectors.<String>toList()`, `Collectors.<String, Integer>groupingBy(x -> x.length())`.
     javac accepts the same calls with the witness, so this is a spelling, not
     a difference in what compiles. */
final class Collectors {
  private Collectors() {
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------- to a collection

  public static <T> Collector<T, List<T>, List<T>> toList() {
    return new CollectorImpl<T, List<T>, List<T>>(
      () -> new ArrayList<T>(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.add(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.addAll(b)
        return a
      },
      CollectorImpl.<List<T>>identity(),
      CollectorChars.identityFinish())
  }

  public static <T> Collector<T, Set<T>, Set<T>> toSet() {
    return new CollectorImpl<T, Set<T>, Set<T>>(
      () -> new HashSet<T>(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.add(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.addAll(b)
        return a
      },
      CollectorImpl.<Set<T>>identity(),
      CollectorChars.identityFinish())
  }

  /* toCollection names its own container type: the factory decides C, and the
     collector answers exactly the type the factory makes, as in the JDK. */
  public static <T, C extends Collection<T>> Collector<T, C, C> toCollection(Supplier<C> collectionFactory) {
    Supplier<C> f = collectionFactory
    return new CollectorImpl<T, C, C>(
      f,
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.add(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.addAll(b)
        return a
      },
      CollectorImpl.<C>identity(),
      CollectorChars.identityFinish())
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ to a map

  /* toMap's two-argument form has no merge function, so a duplicate key is an
     error in Java and is an error here: the accumulator replaces the value,
     and put() answers the one it displaced -- which the JDK turns into
     IllegalStateException. This one overwrites instead of throwing, and the
     three-argument form below is the one that decides what a duplicate means. */
  public static <T, K, V> Collector<T, Map<K, V>, Map<K, V>> toMap(Function<? super T, ? extends K> keyMapper, Function<? super T, ? extends V> valueMapper) {
    Function<T, K> kf = (Function<T, K>) keyMapper
    Function<T, V> vf = (Function<T, V>) valueMapper
    return new CollectorImpl<T, Map<K, V>, Map<K, V>>(
      () -> new HashMap<K, V>(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.put(kf.apply(t), vf.apply(t))
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.putAll(b)
        return a
      },
      CollectorImpl.<Map<K, V>>identity(),
      CollectorChars.identityFinish())
  }

  public static <T, K, V> Collector<T, Map<K, V>, Map<K, V>> toMap(Function<? super T, ? extends K> keyMapper, Function<? super T, ? extends V> valueMapper, BinaryOperator<V> mergeFunction) {
    Function<T, K> kf = (Function<T, K>) keyMapper
    Function<T, V> vf = (Function<T, V>) valueMapper
    BinaryOperator<V> merge = mergeFunction
    return new CollectorImpl<T, Map<K, V>, Map<K, V>>(
      () -> new HashMap<K, V>(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        K key = kf.apply(t)
        V value = vf.apply(t)
        if (acc.containsKey(key)) {
          acc.put(key, merge.apply(acc.get(key), value))
        } else {
          acc.put(key, value)
        }
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        Iterator<K> keys = b.keySet().iterator()
        while (keys.hasNext()) {
          K k = keys.next()
          if (a.containsKey(k)) {
            a.put(k, merge.apply(a.get(k), b.get(k)))
          } else {
            a.put(k, b.get(k))
          }
        }
        return a
      },
      CollectorImpl.<Map<K, V>>identity(),
      CollectorChars.identityFinish())
  }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- joining

  /* Java declares joining over CharSequence, and this dialect has no
     CharSequence: String is the only string type, so the collector is declared
     over String. Every Java program that joins a Stream<String> is unaffected;
     one that joins a Stream<StringBuilder> has no counterpart here. */
  public static Collector<String, StringJoiner, String> joining() {
    return joining("")
  }

  public static Collector<String, StringJoiner, String> joining(String delimiter) {
    String d = delimiter
    return new CollectorImpl<String, StringJoiner, String>(
      () -> new StringJoiner(d),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.add(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.merge(b)
        return a
      },
      sj -> sj.toString(),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  public static Collector<String, StringJoiner, String> joining(String delimiter, String prefix, String suffix) {
    String d = delimiter
    String pre = prefix
    String suf = suffix
    return new CollectorImpl<String, StringJoiner, String>(
      () -> new StringJoiner(d, pre, suf),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.add(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.merge(b)
        return a
      },
      sj -> sj.toString(),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------- counting and summing

  public static <T> Collector<T, LongAccumulator, Long> counting() {
    return new CollectorImpl<T, LongAccumulator, Long>(
      () -> new LongAccumulator(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.value = acc.value + 1L
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.value = a.value + b.value
        return a
      },
      box -> Long.valueOf(box.value),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  public static <T> Collector<T, IntAccumulator, Integer> summingInt(ToIntFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToIntFunction<T> m = (ToIntFunction<T>) mapper
    return new CollectorImpl<T, IntAccumulator, Integer>(
      () -> new IntAccumulator(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.value = acc.value + m.applyAsInt(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.value = a.value + b.value
        return a
      },
      box -> Integer.valueOf(box.value),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  public static <T> Collector<T, LongAccumulator, Long> summingLong(ToLongFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToLongFunction<T> m = (ToLongFunction<T>) mapper
    return new CollectorImpl<T, LongAccumulator, Long>(
      () -> new LongAccumulator(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.value = acc.value + m.applyAsLong(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.value = a.value + b.value
        return a
      },
      box -> Long.valueOf(box.value),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  public static <T> Collector<T, DoubleAccumulator, Double> summingDouble(ToDoubleFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToDoubleFunction<T> m = (ToDoubleFunction<T>) mapper
    return new CollectorImpl<T, DoubleAccumulator, Double>(
      () -> new DoubleAccumulator(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.value = acc.value + m.applyAsDouble(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.value = a.value + b.value
        return a
      },
      box -> Double.valueOf(box.value),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------- averaging

  /* An empty stream averages to 0.0, not to an empty Optional: the
     OptionalDouble is what IntStream.average() answers, this is not. */
  public static <T> Collector<T, AverageAccumulator, Double> averagingInt(ToIntFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToIntFunction<T> m = (ToIntFunction<T>) mapper
    return new CollectorImpl<T, AverageAccumulator, Double>(
      () -> new AverageAccumulator(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.count = acc.count + 1L
        acc.sum = acc.sum + (long) m.applyAsInt(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.count = a.count + b.count
        a.sum = a.sum + b.sum
        return a
      },
      box -> Double.valueOf(box.count == 0 ? 0.0 : (double) box.sum / (double) box.count),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  public static <T> Collector<T, AverageAccumulator, Double> averagingLong(ToLongFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToLongFunction<T> m = (ToLongFunction<T>) mapper
    return new CollectorImpl<T, AverageAccumulator, Double>(
      () -> new AverageAccumulator(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.count = acc.count + 1L
        acc.sum = acc.sum + m.applyAsLong(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.count = a.count + b.count
        a.sum = a.sum + b.sum
        return a
      },
      box -> Double.valueOf(box.count == 0 ? 0.0 : (double) box.sum / (double) box.count),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  public static <T> Collector<T, DoubleAverageAccumulator, Double> averagingDouble(ToDoubleFunction<? super T> mapper) {
    ToDoubleFunction<T> m = (ToDoubleFunction<T>) mapper
    return new CollectorImpl<T, DoubleAverageAccumulator, Double>(
      () -> new DoubleAverageAccumulator(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.count = acc.count + 1L
        acc.sum = acc.sum + m.applyAsDouble(t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.count = a.count + b.count
        a.sum = a.sum + b.sum
        return a
      },
      box -> Double.valueOf(box.count == 0 ? 0.0 : box.sum / (double) box.count),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------- reducing

  public static <T> Collector<T, StreamBox<T>, T> reducing(T identity, BinaryOperator<T> op) {
    T seed = identity
    BinaryOperator<T> f = op
    return new CollectorImpl<T, StreamBox<T>, T>(
      () -> new StreamBox<T>(seed),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.value = f.apply(acc.value, t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.value = f.apply(a.value, b.value)
        return a
      },
      box -> box.value,
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  /* The one-argument reducing answers an Optional, because an empty stream has
     no identity to fall back on. An empty container is the absent case; a
     container holding null is the same thing here, since no element of any
     stream this file builds is null. */
  public static <T> Collector<T, StreamBox<T>, Optional<T>> reducing(BinaryOperator<T> op) {
    BinaryOperator<T> f = op
    return new CollectorImpl<T, StreamBox<T>, Optional<T>>(
      () -> new StreamBox<T>(null),
      (acc, t) -> {
        if (acc.value == null) {
          acc.value = t
        } else {
          acc.value = f.apply(acc.value, t)
        }
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        if (a.value == null) {
          a.value = b.value
        } else if (b.value != null) {
          a.value = f.apply(a.value, b.value)
        }
        return a
      },
      box -> box.value == null ? Optional.<T>empty() : Optional.<T>of(box.value),
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  public static <T, U> Collector<T, StreamBox<U>, U> reducing(U identity, Function<? super T, ? extends U> mapper, BinaryOperator<U> op) {
    U seed = identity
    Function<T, U> m = (Function<T, U>) mapper
    BinaryOperator<U> f = op
    return new CollectorImpl<T, StreamBox<U>, U>(
      () -> new StreamBox<U>(seed),
      (acc, t) -> {
        acc.value = f.apply(acc.value, m.apply(t))
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        a.value = f.apply(a.value, b.value)
        return a
      },
      box -> box.value,
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------- min and max

  /* minBy and maxBy keep the first of equals, which is what a strict
     comparison does -- and what the JDK's do, since they are `reducing` over
     a comparison. */
  public static <T> Collector<T, StreamBox<T>, Optional<T>> minBy(Comparator<? super T> comparator) {
    Comparator<T> c = (Comparator<T>) comparator
    return reducing((a, b) -> c.compare(a, b) <= 0 ? a : b)
  }

  public static <T> Collector<T, StreamBox<T>, Optional<T>> maxBy(Comparator<? super T> comparator) {
    Comparator<T> c = (Comparator<T>) comparator
    return reducing((a, b) -> c.compare(a, b) >= 0 ? a : b)
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------------- composed collectors

  /* mapping: run the downstream collector over each element's mapped value.
     The supplier, combiner and finisher are the downstream's own; only the
     accumulator changes, which is why the finisher's type is untouched. */
  public static <T, U, A, R> Collector<T, A, R> mapping(Function<? super T, ? extends U> mapper, Collector<? super U, A, R> downstream) {
    Function<T, U> m = (Function<T, U>) mapper
    BiConsumer<A, U> sink = downstream.accumulator()
    return new CollectorImpl<T, A, R>(
      downstream.supplier(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        sink.accept(acc, m.apply(t))
      },
      downstream.combiner(),
      downstream.finisher(),
      downstream.characteristics())
  }

  /* groupingBy: one key per element, and each key's elements go to the
     downstream collector. An element's key and that key's container both come
     out of the element, so a group is a Map value holding whatever the
     downstream accumulates.

     The finisher walks the map and finishes each bucket, which is why the
     answer is a different map from the accumulator. Java skips that copy when
     the downstream is IDENTITY_FINISH; the copy is here because a
     Map<K, A> cannot be handed back as a Map<K, R>. */
  public static <T, K> Collector<T, Map<K, List<T>>, Map<K, List<T>>> groupingBy(Function<? super T, ? extends K> classifier) {
    return groupingBy(classifier, Collectors.<T>toList())
  }

  public static <T, K, A, R> Collector<T, Map<K, A>, Map<K, R>> groupingBy(Function<? super T, ? extends K> classifier, Collector<? super T, A, R> downstream) {
    Function<T, K> kf = (Function<T, K>) classifier
    Supplier<A> ds = downstream.supplier()
    BiConsumer<A, ? super T> rawSink = downstream.accumulator()
    BiConsumer<A, T> sink = (BiConsumer<A, T>) rawSink
    BinaryOperator<A> dc = downstream.combiner()
    Function<A, R> df = downstream.finisher()
    return new CollectorImpl<T, Map<K, A>, Map<K, R>>(
      () -> new HashMap<K, A>(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        K key = kf.apply(t)
        A bucket = acc.get(key)
        if (bucket == null) {
          bucket = ds.get()
          acc.put(key, bucket)
        }
        sink.accept(bucket, t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        Iterator<K> keys = b.keySet().iterator()
        while (keys.hasNext()) {
          K k = keys.next()
          A other = b.get(k)
          A mine = a.get(k)
          if (mine == null) {
            a.put(k, other)
          } else {
            a.put(k, dc.apply(mine, other))
          }
        }
        return a
      },
      m -> {
        Map<K, R> out = new HashMap<K, R>()
        Iterator<K> keys = m.keySet().iterator()
        while (keys.hasNext()) {
          K k = keys.next()
          out.put(k, df.apply(m.get(k)))
        }
        return out
      },
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  /* The three-argument form takes a factory for the map itself. Its type
     arguments cannot all be read off its arguments -- which map to make is the
     factory's business and the value type belongs to the downstream -- so the
     call site names them:

         Collectors.<String, Integer, List<String>, List<String>, TreeMap<Integer, List<String>>>groupingBy(x -> x.length(), TreeMap::new, Collectors.<String>toList())

     which is the call javac needs a witness for as well. */
  public static <T, K, A, R, M extends Map<K, R>> Collector<T, Map<K, A>, M> groupingBy(Function<? super T, ? extends K> classifier, Supplier<M> mapFactory, Collector<? super T, A, R> downstream) {
    Function<T, K> kf = (Function<T, K>) classifier
    Supplier<M> factory = mapFactory
    Supplier<A> ds = downstream.supplier()
    BiConsumer<A, ? super T> rawSink = downstream.accumulator()
    BiConsumer<A, T> sink = (BiConsumer<A, T>) rawSink
    BinaryOperator<A> dc = downstream.combiner()
    Function<A, R> df = downstream.finisher()
    return new CollectorImpl<T, Map<K, A>, M>(
      () -> new HashMap<K, A>(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        K key = kf.apply(t)
        A bucket = acc.get(key)
        if (bucket == null) {
          bucket = ds.get()
          acc.put(key, bucket)
        }
        sink.accept(bucket, t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        Iterator<K> keys = b.keySet().iterator()
        while (keys.hasNext()) {
          K k = keys.next()
          A other = b.get(k)
          A mine = a.get(k)
          if (mine == null) {
            a.put(k, other)
          } else {
            a.put(k, dc.apply(mine, other))
          }
        }
        return a
      },
      m -> {
        M out = factory.get()
        Iterator<K> keys = m.keySet().iterator()
        while (keys.hasNext()) {
          K k = keys.next()
          out.put(k, df.apply(m.get(k)))
        }
        return out
      },
      CollectorChars.none())
  }

  /* partitioningBy is a groupingBy whose key is a boolean, with one difference
     the JDK documents and this keeps: the answer always has both keys. An all-
     true stream partitions to {false=[], true=[...]}, not to a one-entry map,
     which is what lets a caller read either side without checking. */
  public static <T> Collector<T, Map<Boolean, List<T>>, Map<Boolean, List<T>>> partitioningBy(Predicate<? super T> predicate) {
    return partitioningBy(predicate, Collectors.<T>toList())
  }

  public static <T, A, R> Collector<T, Map<Boolean, A>, Map<Boolean, R>> partitioningBy(Predicate<? super T> predicate, Collector<? super T, A, R> downstream) {
    Predicate<T> p = (Predicate<T>) predicate
    Supplier<A> ds = downstream.supplier()
    BiConsumer<A, ? super T> rawSink = downstream.accumulator()
    BiConsumer<A, T> sink = (BiConsumer<A, T>) rawSink
    BinaryOperator<A> dc = downstream.combiner()
    Function<A, R> df = downstream.finisher()
    return new CollectorImpl<T, Map<Boolean, A>, Map<Boolean, R>>(
      () -> new HashMap<Boolean, A>(),
      (acc, t) -> {
        Boolean key = p.test(t) ? Boolean.TRUE : Boolean.FALSE
        A bucket = acc.get(key)
        if (bucket == null) {
          bucket = ds.get()
          acc.put(key, bucket)
        }
        sink.accept(bucket, t)
      },
      (a, b) -> {
        Iterator<Boolean> keys = b.keySet().iterator()
        while (keys.hasNext()) {
          Boolean k = keys.next()
          A other = b.get(k)
          A mine = a.get(k)
          if (mine == null) {
            a.put(k, other)
          } else {
            a.put(k, dc.apply(mine, other))
          }
        }
        return a
      },
      m -> {
        Map<Boolean, R> out = new HashMap<Boolean, R>()
        A yes = m.get(Boolean.TRUE)
        A no = m.get(Boolean.FALSE)
        out.put(Boolean.TRUE, df.apply(yes == null ? ds.get() : yes))
        out.put(Boolean.FALSE, df.apply(no == null ? ds.get() : no))
        return out
      },
      CollectorChars.none())
  }
}
